.TH ZEDIT 1 "2026-07-26" "zedit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zedit \- a fast, terminal-based, modal code editor written in Zig
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zedit
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zedit
(\(lqzig-editor\(rq) is a modal terminal editor in the spirit of
.BR nvim (1)
and
.BR hx (1).
It is non-modal out of the box, with VS Code's key bindings; setting
.B keymap
to
.B vim
gives a comprehensive vim-style keymap instead.
It has multiple cursors, fuzzy file and
global-content search pickers, syntax highlighting, and an AstroNvim/Helix-style
themed interface \(em all with no external runtime dependencies.
.PP
If
.I file
exists it is opened; otherwise a buffer with that name is created on the first
write. If
.I file
is a directory, zedit enters it and starts in the fuzzy file picker. With no
.I file
argument an empty buffer is opened. The editor requires an interactive terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version (from the VERSION file; see CHANGELOG.md) and exit.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log " " \fIpath\fP
Write timestamped diagnostic logs (including microsecond render/input timings)
to
.IR path .
Logging is off by default.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-lsp " " \fIcmd\fP
Use
.I cmd
as the language server for the opened file instead of the per-filetype default.
An empty
.I cmd
starts no server at all, whatever is installed.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-dap " " \fIcmd\fP
Use
.I cmd
as the debug adapter instead of the per-filetype default
.RB ( lldb-dap ", " debugpy ", " "dlv dap" ).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIpath\fP
Read configuration from
.I path
instead of the standard location (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keymap " " \fIname\fP
Use the
.BR vscode ", " zed " or " vim
key bindings for this run, whatever the configuration file says (see
.B keymap
under
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-check\-update
Compare this build with the newest published release tag (one
.B git ls\-remote
call) and exit. zedit never checks on its own;
.B :update
does the same from inside the editor.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-benchmark
Time the hot paths (open, whole-buffer literal and regex search, serialize) on
.I file
\(em or on 10 MB of synthetic text when no file is given \(em print a report
and exit. Runs headless; the comparative suite against helix/nvim is
.BR "zig build bench" .
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Write the fully documented default configuration file to the standard location
and exit. Refuses to overwrite an existing file.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Reset the configuration to the documented defaults and exit, keeping whatever
was there as
.I config.bak
\(em a reset the user asked for should still not be the moment their settings
vanish. Unlike
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
this does overwrite.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tutor
Open the interactive tutorial (in the tradition of
.BR vimtutor (1)).
The tutor is a scratch buffer: edit it freely.
.SH CONFIGURATION
zedit reads a single configuration file at
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zedit/config
(falling back to
.IR ~/.config/zedit/config ).
The format is one
.B key = value
per line;
.B #
starts a comment; unknown keys are ignored; a missing file means defaults.
Generate a fully documented copy with
.BR "zedit \-\-init\-config" .
.TP
.B theme
Colour theme:
.BR tokyonight " (default), " gruvbox ", " catppuccin ", " nord ", " onedark .
Switch at runtime with
.B :theme
or the
.B Space f t
picker.
.TP
.B keymap
.BR vscode " (default), " zed " or " vim .
The first two make the editor \fInon-modal\fP: typing always
inserts, and the commands live on chords \(em
.BR Ctrl-s " save, " Ctrl-p " files, " Ctrl-f " find, " Ctrl-h " replace,"
.BR Ctrl-z / Ctrl-y " undo/redo, " Ctrl-c / Ctrl-x / Ctrl-v " clipboard,"
.BR Ctrl-a " select all, " Ctrl-/ " comment, " Ctrl-b " explorer,"
.B Ctrl-w
close \(em with Shift+arrows selecting, Ctrl+arrows moving by word, and
Alt+Up/Down moving a line (add Shift to copy it).
.B Ctrl-d
selects the word under the cursor, and each further press adds a caret at the
next occurrence, so typing edits them all at once.
.B Ctrl-p
then
.B >
opens the command palette.
.B zed
is the same table under its own name.
.B vim
is modal editing, and is what the rest of this page documents.
.PP
It is an emulation, not a hybrid: the vim commands are not reachable under
them. Two limits are worth knowing. A selection includes the cell under the
caret (zedit's visual model), so it is one character wider at that end than
VS Code's. And
.BI Ctrl+Shift+ letter
chords cannot work at all, because a terminal does not pass the shift bit for
them \(em so the command palette is reached the way VS Code also offers,
.B Ctrl-p
then
.BR > ,
and project search has no key of its own.
.TP
.B tab_width
Cells a tab occupies when rendered (1\-16, default 4). Tabs are stored
verbatim.
.TP
.B nerd_font
.BR true / false
(default true). The powerline statusline separators are Nerd Font private-use
glyphs; set
.B false
for a flat statusline that renders correctly in any font.
.TP
.B sidebar
.BR left " (default) or " right :
which side the file-tree explorer opens on.
.TP
.B relative_numbers
.BR true / false
(default true). Relative line numbers in the gutter, with the cursor line
absolute (the AstroNvim hybrid style); set
.B false
for absolute numbers everywhere.
.TP
.B large_file_mb
Files larger than this many megabytes (default 64) open in large-file mode:
highlighting, LSP and git signs are skipped so huge files open instantly.
.TP
.B autoindent
.BR true / false
(default true).
.BR o / O /Enter/ cc
inherit the current line's indentation; an auto\-indent left blank is
stripped, like vim. Where the grammar ships an indent query (Zig, C, Python,
Rust, Go, JavaScript, TypeScript) the syntax tree takes over: the new line
inherits from the line it follows and gains one level per block that line
opens, so Enter after
.B {
or
.B "def f():"
steps in. Files with no grammar — and a blank line to follow, which carries no
indent and opens nothing — keep vim's plain copy rule.
.TP
.B buffer_tabs
.BR true / false
(default true). Show the title bar across the top: one powerline tab per open
buffer (the active one accent\-coloured, unsaved ones dotted) beside the
explorer's header when the sidebar is open. Always on screen while enabled,
even for a single file;
.B false
removes the row and puts the filename back in the statusline.
.TP
.B auto_completion
.BR true / false
(default true). Request completions while typing an identifier, after a pause,
instead of only on
.BR Ctrl\-n .
The list is fuzzy\-matched and ranked, so
.B mplt
finds
.BR mockComplete .
.TP
.B completion_delay_ms
Pause before an automatic completion request (default 150). The timer is armed
only while typing, so an idle editor still blocks in
.BR poll (2)
and uses no CPU.
.TP
.B buffer_completion
.BR true / false
(default true). When no language server answers \(em none is installed for the
filetype, or it returned nothing \(em fill the completion popup from the
identifiers already in the open buffers (vim's keyword completion). The word
being typed is never offered as its own completion.
.B false
completes only from a server.
.TP
.B wrap_indent
(default true). Repeat a wrapped line's indent in front of every continuation
row, so it hangs under its own first character. Capped at half the window.
.TP
.B wrap_column
(default 0). Wrap at this column instead of at the window edge; 0 means the
window edge, and a value wider than the window is clamped to it.
.TP
.B persistent_undo
(default false). Keep the undo tree on disk, under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/zedit/undo ,
so undo still works after reopening a file. It holds the edits, not a copy of
the text, and is created readable only by you; it is ignored if the file has
been changed by something else since. Whenever an undo file is written,
sibling undo files untouched for 90 days are removed (each removal is
logged), so the state directory does not grow forever.
.TP
.B sync_background
(default true). Paint the terminal's window background \(em the few pixels
of padding outside the character grid, seen as a strip along the bottom and
right edges of the window \(em in the theme's background colour, so it
matches the editor instead of showing through in the terminal's own colour.
zedit asks the terminal for its current background first and restores it on
exit, so a terminal that does not answer is never left recoloured.
.TP
.B split_sizes
(default empty). Relative window sizes, applied when a split has exactly
this many windows: \fI1,2\fR makes the second window twice the first.
Being relative they hold at any terminal size. Empty tiles evenly.
.B :winsave
writes the current proportions here.
.TP
.B soft_wrap
(default true). Continue a line too long for the window on the next screen
row, marked with a dim arrow in the gutter, instead of scrolling the view
sideways. Set false for horizontal scrolling.
.TP
.B inline_diagnostics
.BR true / false
(default true). Show each diagnostic's message inline at the end of its line,
dimmed and coloured by severity. The gutter sign and the statusline message
appear either way.
.TP
.B format_on_save
.BR true / false
(default true). Ask the language server to format the document before every
.BR :w ,
when the server advertises formatting;
.B :format
formats on demand.
.TP
.B cmdline_suggestions
.BR true / false
(default true). Inline suggestions on the command line (fish\-style): the
rest of the newest matching history entry, or a command name, appears as dim
ghost text after the cursor;
.B Right
or
.B End
accepts it. Enter always runs only what you typed. Set
.B false
to turn the ghost text off.
.TP
.B mouse
.BR true / false
(default true). Ask the terminal to report mouse events: the wheel scrolls, a
click moves the cursor (and focuses the split it landed in), and a drag
selects. Set
.B false
to leave the mouse entirely to your terminal \(em including its own click\-drag
selection, which any reporting mode takes over. See
.BR MOUSE .
.TP
.B mousetime
.I milliseconds
(default 500). How far apart two clicks at the same cell may be and still
count as a double click \(em then triple, then quadruple \(em as in vim's
.BR 'mousetime' .
The count is worked out from the previous click when the next one arrives, so
no timer runs.
.B 0
turns multi\-clicks off.
.SH SNIPPETS
A completion item the server marks as a snippet expands its placeholders and
starts a tabstop session:
.B Tab
and
.B Shift\-Tab
move between the stops, the first character typed at an untouched placeholder
replaces it, and
.B Esc
ends the session leaving ordinary text. Stops follow your edits, including a
line you split with
.BR Enter .
A choice placeholder
.RB ( "${1|a,b,c|}" )
inserts the first alternative and cycles the rest with
.B Ctrl\-n
/
.BR Ctrl\-p . Items carrying a
.B textEdit
replace the server's own range, and any
.B additionalTextEdits
(auto\-imports) are applied with them.
.SH "PARTIAL COMMANDS"
The command being typed appears at the right of the statusline, and the
finished command stays there until the next one begins \(em
.BR d ,
.BR di ,
.BR 2d ,
.BR \(dqay ,
.B ^W
\(em vim's
.BR showcmd .
A macro recording marker
.RB ( REC\ @a )
shares the same slot.
.SH MODES
zedit is modal: NORMAL for movement and commands, INSERT for typing, VISUAL and
V\-LINE and V\-BLOCK for selections, COMMAND for the
.RB \(lq : \(rq
line, and PICKER for the fuzzy finders.
.B Esc
returns to NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B gj gk g0 g$
Move by screen row rather than buffer line: down, up, to the start and to
the end of the row the cursor is on. With
.B soft_wrap
off, or on a line that fits, they are exactly
.BR j ", " k ", " 0 " and " $ .
.TP
.B { }
Previous / next paragraph (an empty line). Both are jump motions, so
.B Ctrl\-o
returns.
.TP
.B ip ap
Inner / around paragraph text objects (linewise).
.B ap
also takes the trailing blank lines \(em or the leading ones when nothing
trails, like vim. Counts work:
.BR d2ap .
.SH "REMOTE FILES (SSH)"
.B zedit ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path
edits a file on another machine, as do
.B :e ssh://\&...
and
.BR ":ssh [user@]host[/dir]" .
Nothing is installed on the remote: each operation is a single
.BR ssh (1)
invocation, so any host you can already ssh into works, including aliases from
.IR ~/.ssh/config .
A remote directory opens the fuzzy picker over its files, and
.B :w
writes back to the same URL \(em into a temporary file beside the target,
renamed into place in the same invocation, so a transfer that fails partway
never leaves the file half\-written. Remote paths are quoted for the remote
shell, so spaces and quotes are safe.
.B BatchMode=yes
is set, so a host that would prompt for a password fails fast instead of
hanging the editor \(em use keys or an agent.
.SH "STARTUP SCREEN"
Started with no file, zedit lists recently opened files and directories:
.B j
/
.B k
(or the arrows) select,
.B Enter
opens,
.BR 1 \- 9
jump straight to an entry,
.B q
quits, and any other key dismisses the screen. The list is kept in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/zedit/recent
(default
.IR ~/.local/state/zedit/recent ),
newest first, capped at 30 entries, with vanished paths pruned on load.
.SH "LANGUAGE INJECTIONS"
A region written in another language is parsed by that language's grammar and
highlighted with its own colours: a fenced code block in Markdown uses the
language named by its info string when that is one of the vendored grammars
(zig, c, python, json, javascript, typescript, rust, go, html, markdown), and
the body of an HTML
.B <script>
element is JavaScript. Only the visible part of the document is scanned, so a
fenced block below the fold costs nothing until it is scrolled to. There is no
CSS grammar, so a
.B <style>
body is left as plain HTML.
.SH "STRUCTURAL OBJECTS (tree-sitter)"
.TP
.B af if
The function around the cursor \(em whole, or just its body.
.TP
.B ac ic
The class, struct, impl or enum around the cursor.
.TP
.B aa ia
The argument or parameter around the cursor.
.B aa
also takes the comma joining it to its neighbour \(em the one after it, or
the one before it for the last argument \(em so what is left is still a
well-formed list.
.TP
.B aC iC
The comment around the cursor.
.B aC
takes the whole comment, extending over a run of comment lines at the same
column, and is linewise when the comment owns its lines;
.B iC
is just the text, without the delimiter.
.TP
.B ]f [f
Jump to the next or previous function. Both are jump motions, so
.B Ctrl\-o
returns.
.PP
These read the real syntax tree, so they follow the language's grammar rather
than brace counting. Files whose language has no vendored grammar have no
structural objects.
.SH MOUSE
zedit asks the terminal for button and drag reports (DEC modes 1002 + 1006).
Set
.B mouse = false
in the configuration file to ask for nothing at all, leaving the mouse
entirely to your terminal.
.PP
A left click in a window's text area moves the cursor there; if that window is
not the focused one, the click focuses it first. Nothing is added to the
jumplist, a pending count is discarded, an open selection ends, and insert
mode continues at the new position \(em all as in Neovim's
.BR mouse=a .
With an
.I operator
pending, the click is its motion: an exclusive charwise range, which becomes
linewise by vim's usual rule when it ends in column 0. Clicks resolve
correctly through soft-wrapped continuation rows (indent included), tabs,
double-width characters, inlay hints, the diff views' virtual rows and the
gutter (which counts as column 1).
.PP
Holding the button and moving selects: the press anchors, each crossed cell
extends, and the release finishes, leaving an ordinary charwise visual
selection that
.BR d ,
.BR y ,
.B c
and the rest act on. A drag that leaves the window keeps extending inside it,
clamped to what is on screen. A drag that never leaves its cell stays a plain
click. Alt+drag blockwise is not implemented; nor are modified clicks
generally, which stay inert rather than pretending to be plain ones.
.PP
Clicking the same cell again inside
.B mousetime
(500 ms by default) counts up, as in vim: two clicks select the
.IR word ,
three the whole
.I line
(linewise, newline included, whatever column you hit), four one blockwise
cell, and the fifth starts the cycle over. The count is derived from the
previous click's time and cell when the next press arrives, so no timer runs
and an idle editor still costs nothing; one column off, or one millisecond
late, and the chain restarts. Every press counts, wherever it lands, so a
click on a status line, the command line, the title bar or the file tree
between two clicks leaves the second one a plain click.
.PP
The word a double click takes is vim's
.I mouse
word, not
.BR iw .
Blanks and keyword characters take their run. On punctuation, vim first tries
.BR % :
if there is a bracket at or after the click on that line, the selection runs
from the click to its match \(em backwards, or onto another line, if that is
where the match is. Otherwise punctuation groups only with its own class, so
.B \->
and
.B *=
select as one while
.B .,;
selects one character at a time. Multibyte text follows vim's character
classes: CJK does not group with the Latin word beside it, accented letters
do. A click past the end of a line clamps to its last word.
.PP
Holding the button down after a multi\-click drags in the same units: whole
words, whole lines, or a real rectangle, in either direction, with the word
you clicked always kept whole.
.PP
A gesture begun in insert mode is Neovim's
.IR "Insert Visual" :
the statusline reads
.BR "(insert) VISUAL" ,
and whatever ends the selection \(em
.BR Esc ,
.BR v ,
an operator, or a plain click \(em returns to insert where it left the cursor,
so typing simply continues. A second
.B Esc
leaves insert.
.PP
The wheel scrolls the window
.I under the pointer
\(em focused or not, and it never moves focus \(em three lines per notch,
moving that window's cursor with it so it keeps its position on screen. At the
very top or bottom of the file nothing moves. A window's status line counts as
part of that window, so a notch there scrolls it even when the focus is
elsewhere; over the file tree, the title bar or the command line \(em which
belong to no window \(em the focused window scrolls. In a picker the
wheel scrolls the preview
pane. Clicking a buffer tab in the title bar switches to it. In the file-tree
sidebar a single click on a row selects it and acts as Enter does: a
directory expands or collapses, a file opens with focus back in the buffer;
clicking the EXPLORER header or the space below the tree focuses the tree.
The mouse works inside a picker too (including the view
.B zedit \fIdirectory\fP
starts in): a click on a result row selects it and the preview follows, a
click on the already-selected row opens it \(em so a double-click opens from
anywhere \(em explorer and tab clicks keep working (a tab click closes the
picker and lands on that buffer), and the prompt row and preview pane are
ignored. A jump further
than half a window \(em
.BR G ,
.BR 100G ,
a search hit \(em redraws with the cursor centred, as vim does.
.PP
To select text for your
.I terminal's
own clipboard, use its
.B Shift
+drag. Every mouse-tracking mode takes the plain button away from the
terminal, so Shift is the escape hatch in any of them \(em and Shift+click,
Shift+drag and Shift+wheel therefore never reach zedit at all.
.SH "NORMAL MODE KEYS"
.SS Motions
.TP
.B h j k l
Left, down, up, right (arrow keys also work, counts included).
.TP
.B w W b B e E
Word / WORD motions.
.TP
.B 0 ^ $
Start, first non-blank, end of line.
.TP
.B gg G \fItag\fP{n}G
First line, last line, line \fIn\fP.
.TP
.B f F t T \fR;\fP ,
Find/till a character on the line, repeat and reverse.
.TP
.B %
Jump to the matching bracket.
.TP
.B H M L
Top, middle, bottom of the screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl-d Ctrl-u Ctrl-f Ctrl-b
Half/full page down and up.
.SS Operators
.TP
.B d c y
Delete, change, yank \(em followed by a motion or text object, or doubled
.RB ( dd ", " cc ", " yy ).
.TP
.B > <
Indent and dedent
.RB ( >> ", " << ).
.TP
.B gc\fItag\fP{motion} gcc
Toggle comments.
.TP
.B D C Y x X s S r ~ J
Delete/change to end of line, delete char, substitute, replace char, toggle
case, join lines.
.TP
.B R
Replace mode: typed characters overwrite what is already there instead of
pushing it right, and
.B Backspace
puts back exactly what each keystroke covered. Past the end of the line typing
appends. A count types the text that many times
.RB ( 3Rab
leaves
.BR ababab ),
and the whole session is one undo step.
.TP
.B gR
Virtual replace: as
.BR R ,
except a keystroke covers display \fIcolumns\fP rather than characters. Typing
over a tab shrinks it instead of destroying it, and the tab goes only once
every column it drew has been covered. Backspace puts back whatever the
keystroke covered, a swallowed tab included. With no tab in reach it behaves
exactly like
.BR R .
.TP
.B gu\fR{motion}\fP gU\fR{motion}\fP g~\fR{motion}\fP
Lowercase, uppercase and toggle the case of the text a motion covers; doubled
.RB ( guu ", " gUU ", " g~~ )
for the whole line.
.TP
.B gJ
Join lines without a separator \(em unlike
.BR J ,
it neither strips the next line's indent nor inserts a space.
.SS "Text objects"
.TP
.B iw aw i( a( i" a" \fR(and\fP i[ a{ i< i' i`\fR)\fP
Inner/around word, brackets, and quotes, e.g.
.BR ciw ", " di" ", " da( .
.SS Surround
.TP
.B ys\fItag\fP{motion}{char}
Surround the motion's text with {char}, e.g.
.B ysiw)
wraps the inner word in parentheses.
.TP
.B cs{old}{new}
Change surrounding {old} to {new}, e.g.
.BR cs"' .
.TP
.B ds{char}
Delete the surrounding {char}, e.g.
.BR ds( .
.SS "Registers, undo, repeat"
.TP
.B \(dq{a-z} \(dq+ \(dq*
Select a register for the next yank/delete/paste.
.B \(dq+
and
.B \(dq*
are the system clipboard: yanking to them emits OSC 52, which sets the local
clipboard even over SSH. Terminal pastes (bracketed paste) insert literally.
.TP
.B p P u Ctrl-r .
Paste after/before, undo, redo, repeat last change. Undo history is a tree: a
change made after an undo starts a branch rather than discarding what was
undone.
.TP
.B g\- g+
Go to the previous / next state in the order the states were made, across
branches \(em so a change stranded by an undo followed by a new edit can be
reached again. Counts work:
.BR 3g\- .
.TP
.B :earlier " / " :later
The same from the command line, with a count
.RB ( ":earlier 2" )
a span
.RB ( ":earlier 10s" ", " 2m ", " 1h )
or a number of file writes
.RB ( ":earlier 1f" ", " 2f ").
Asking for more than there is goes to the oldest (or newest) state.
.TP
.B :undolist
Every state in a picker \(em the current one marked, branch points flagged.
Enter jumps to the state chosen.
.TP
.B Ctrl-o Ctrl-i
Jumplist: back / forward through jump\-motions (searches, \fBG\fP, marks,
\fBgd\fP, buffer switches \(em across buffers).
.SS "Multiple cursors"
.TP
.B Ctrl-n Ctrl-p
Add a cursor on the line below / above. Movement,
.BR x ,
and
.BR i / a / I / A
plus typing apply to every cursor;
.B Esc
collapses to one.
.SS "Marks and macros"
.TP
.B m{a-z} `{a-z} '{a-z}
Set a mark, jump to it exactly, jump to its line.
.TP
.B q{a-z} \fR...\fP q \fR then\fP @{a-z}
Record and replay a macro.
.B @@
replays the last one, and both take a count. A replay stops at the first
command that fails \(em a motion with nowhere to go, or a find or search
with no match \(em rather than running the keys after it.
.TP
.B zz zt zb
Put the cursor's line at the centre, the top or the bottom of the window,
without moving the cursor itself.
.TP
.B gx
Open the URL or file name under the cursor with the desktop's own handler
.RB ( xdg-open ,
or
.B open
on macOS).
.TP
.B gv
Reselect the previous visual selection; used from visual mode it swaps the
two.
.TP
.B "ge gE g_ g^ gm gM go"
Back to the end of the previous word (\fBgE\fP by WORD); the last non\-blank
of the line \fIcount\fP\-1 lower; the first non\-blank of the screen row; the
middle of the window and of the line's own text; and byte \fIN\fP of the
buffer, counting the line break that ends each line.
.TP
.B "g' g\`"
Jump to a mark without recording a jumplist entry \(em otherwise exactly
.B '
and
.BR \` .
.TP
.B "g* g#"
Search for the word under the cursor forward or backward, \fIwithout\fP the
whole\-word bounds that
.B *
and
.B #
add \(em so \fBfoo\fP also finds \fBfoobar\fP.
.TP
.B "gn gN"
Select the next (previous) match of the last search, so an operator acts on
it:
.B dgn
deletes it and
.B .
moves on to the one after.
.TP
.B g?\fR{motion}\fP
Rot13 the text a motion covers;
.B g??
and
.B g?g?
take the line.
.TP
.B gI
Insert at column 1, whatever the indentation.
.TP
.B "gp gP"
Paste as
.B p
and
.BR P ,
but leave the cursor after what was pasted, so repeated
.B gp
stacks.
.TP
.B g&
Run the last
.B :s
again over every line, keeping its flags.
.TP
.B g8
Show the UTF\-8 bytes of the character under the cursor, in hex.
.TP
.B "gf gF"
Open the file whose name is under the cursor, relative to the file being
edited.
.B gF
also honours a trailing
.BI : line \fR.\fP
.TP
.B "g; g,"
Walk the change list backwards and forwards \(em one entry per line changed,
so a session of scattered edits is retraced without marks.
.TP
.B gq\fR{motion}\fP\fR, \fPgw\fR{motion}\fP
Reflow the lines a motion covers to
.B wrap_column
(79 when it is 0). Words are never split and the first line's indent is kept
on every line produced.
.B gq
ends on the last line it made;
.B gw
puts the cursor back where it started.
.TP
.B gD
Go to the declaration of the symbol under the cursor (LSP).
.TP
.B "g CTRL-G"
Report the cursor's line, column and byte offset.
.SH "VISUAL MODE"
.B v
character\-wise,
.B V
line\-wise, and
.B Ctrl-v
block\-wise. Move to extend the selection, then
.BR d / c / y / x / > / < .
In block mode,
.B I
and
.B A
insert at the left/right edge of every selected line:
.B A
pads a line too short to reach the append column with spaces, while
.B I
and
.B c
skip such a line altogether (vim's asymmetry).
.B $
extends the block to each line's own end, and keeps doing so as
.BR j / k
grow it, so
.B $A
appends at every line's end. The block's edges follow display columns and
cover a double\-width character (or a tab) whole. A blockwise
.BR y / d / x
fills the register as a rectangle, which
.BR p / P
paste back as one \(em padding short lines, growing the file past its end,
and laying the block side by side under a count. Press
.B S{char}
to surround the selection.
.TP
.B "CTRL-A CTRL-X \fR(and\fP g CTRL-A\fR,\fP g CTRL-X\fR)\fP"
Add or subtract from the number on every selected line. The
.B g
forms step the amount line by line, so a column of
.B 1
becomes 2, 3, 4 \(em handy for renumbering.
.TP
.B "D X Y C R"
Delete, yank or change whole \fIlines\fP, however the selection was made.
.TP
.B "J r gq O"
Join the selected lines; replace every character in the selection; reflow it;
and, in blockwise, swap the block's horizontal corner.
.TP
.B "v V CTRL-V CTRL-C"
Each of the first three switches to its own kind, or stops Visual mode when it
is already that kind.
.B CTRL-C
stops it outright.
.TP
.B !\fIcommand\fP
Filter the selected lines through \fIcommand\fP \(em
.B !sort
being the classic. Equivalent to
.BI :[range]! command \fR.\fP
.SH "PICKERS (Space leader, AstroNvim-style groups)"
Every picker shares one layout: the file tree on its side (when open), the
results beside it, and a live preview of the selected file on the right,
tree\-sitter highlighted and scrolled to the matching line.
.B Ctrl\-d
and
.B Ctrl\-u
(or the mouse wheel) scroll the preview itself. Starting zedit on a
directory opens this view directly. Remote entries and narrow terminals skip
the preview and give the results the full width.
Pressing
.B Space
shows a which\-key popup; the
.BR b ,
.BR f ,
.B g
and
.B l
groups open a nested popup of their own.
.TP
.B Space f f
Fuzzy file finder (file names). The file list is cached per session;
.B Ctrl-r
inside the picker re\-walks the project. When no file name matches the query,
a dim hint row points at
.B Space f w
(the content search). Starting zedit on a directory shows the same
distinction once in the status line.
.TP
.B Space f w
Global search across file \fIcontents\fP (find words). The query is a regex
with the same modern syntax as
.BR / ,
matched per line; a plain string stays on the literal fast path. Unlike the
fuzzy pickers a space here is a literal character \(em the query is one
regex, so
.B foo.*bar
is the way to ask for order. While the
pattern is incomplete (a lone parenthesis, a trailing backslash) the previous
results stay and the prompt shows a dim tag.
.TP
.B Space f b
Open-buffer picker.
.TP
.B Space f t
Theme picker.
.TP
.B Space f C
The command palette \(em a searchable list of every command with the key
that runs it. The binding shown is the one that works under the keymap in
force, so nothing here names a key you cannot press. Filtering matches the
title
.I and
the command's spelling, so
.B vsplit
finds "Split window vertically"; a command that takes an argument
.RB ( :theme ", " :edit ", " :earlier )
puts itself on the command line rather than guessing one. Typing
.B >
as the first character in the file picker opens the same list \(em VS Code's
Quick Open prefix, and the route to it under the non\-modal keymap, where
.B Ctrl+Shift+P
cannot reach the application.
.TP
.B Space l a\fR, \fPSpace l r\fR, \fPSpace l R\fR, \fPSpace l s\fR, \fPSpace l S\fR, \fPSpace l d\fR, \fPSpace l D\fR, \fPSpace l f\fR, \fPSpace l p\fR, \fPSpace l P
Code action, rename symbol, references (picker), document symbols, workspace
symbols (the server matches the query across the project), line diagnostic,
every diagnostic in the open buffers, format document, and
.I peek
the definition or the references \(em VS Code's
.BR Alt+F12 " and " Shift+F12 ,
neither of which a terminal can deliver. The peek is a floating window over the file
you are reading, syntax\-highlighted and centred on the definition:
.B Enter
takes the jump for real,
.BR Esc " or " q
close it,
.BR j / k ", " Ctrl-d / Ctrl-u
and the wheel scroll it, and a click outside dismisses it. Nothing is opened
and no jumplist entry is made unless you press
.BR Enter .
.IP
Peeked \fIreferences\fP are the same window holding more than one place: the
title counts them,
.BR n / p
(or
.BR Tab / Shift-Tab )
step and the view follows, wrapping at both ends, and
.B Ctrl-q
sends the whole set to the quickfix list. It does not draw VS Code's side
list of the references \(em
.B Space l R
is that list, with a fuzzy prompt and a preview. Use
.B l R
to find one among many and
.B l P
to read them where they are. Rename and code-action edits apply across all the
files they touch (unopened ones load into background buffers;
.B :wa
saves them).
.TP
.B Space g d\fR, \fPSpace g s\fR, \fPSpace g l
Git: unified diff of the current file in a read\-only split (also
.BR :diff ),
the index version side by side (also
.BR :vdiff ),
and the line\-by\-line view (also
.BR :ldiff ).
In the side\-by\-side view focus stays on the worktree pane (the file you
edit), the cursor keeps its place, and the index pane is a read\-only
snapshot. The panes are row\-aligned:
where one side has lines the other lacks, the shorter side shows tinted
virtual filler rows (green for additions, red for deletions; changed lines
are tinted in both panes), and the two panes scroll as one. Soft wrap is off
inside the paired panes \(em a wrapped line would break the row alignment;
horizontal scrolling still works there. The line\-by\-line view opens no
split at all: each hunk's old lines render as red virtual rows woven into
the file's own window above the lines that replaced them (a dim \- in the
gutter, no line number, never under the cursor \(em motions step across
real lines only), added and changed lines tint on their real rows, and the
buffer stays fully editable; like the gutter signs, the woven rows reflect
the file as last saved and refresh on
.BR :w .
Pressing the same key again \(em from
either pane \(em closes its view, and the three views are exclusive per file:
opening one closes the others first. A file with no changes reports so instead
of opening an empty view.
.TP
.B Space e
The file-tree explorer, as a three-state cycle (VS Code's): closed, it opens
with focus; open but unfocused, it takes focus back (selection and scroll
untouched); focused, it closes. Its header lives in the title bar; while the
tree is open, switching buffers reveals the active file in it (ancestor
directories expand and its row is selected). Inside it:
.B j k
move,
.B Enter\fR/\fPl\fR/\fPRight
expand a directory or open the file,
.B h\fR/\fPLeft
collapse / go to parent,
.B a
new file,
.B A
new folder (both prompt on the command line, pre-filled with the selected
row's directory; any missing directories in the name are created, as
.B :w
to such a path does),
.B R
refresh,
.B Esc
return focus to the buffer,
.B q
close.
.TP
.B Space d
The debugger:
.B b
toggle a breakpoint on this line,
.B B
clear them all,
.B c
start or continue,
.B n
step over,
.B i
step into,
.B o
step out,
.B q
end the session. Start one with
.BR ":debug <program> [args]" ;
the adapter comes from
.B --dap
or a per-filetype default. Breakpoints belong to the file, not the session, so
they survive the program exiting.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-` " (also " Ctrl-Space ")"
Toggle the embedded terminal open and shut, from either mode — the two send
the same byte and cannot be told apart.
.TP
.B Space t
An embedded terminal: a shell on its own pty in a horizontal split, opened in
Terminal mode so typing goes to it.
.B Ctrl-\e\\ Ctrl-n
returns to normal mode and
.B i
goes back in. A second
.B Space t
focuses the shell already open rather than starting another; an exited shell
keeps its last output until any key closes the window. Output that scrolls off
the top is kept: 5000 rows of scrollback, paged with the wheel in Terminal mode
or
.B Ctrl-u
and
.B Ctrl-d
in normal mode. Also
.BR :terminal .
.TP
.B "z<CR> z. z-"
As
.BR zt ", " zz " and " zb ,
but also to the line's first non\-blank.
.TP
.B "z+ z^"
Start on the line below (above) the window, then behave like
.B z<CR>
and
.BR z- .
.TP
.B "zh zl zH zL zs ze z<Left> z<Right>"
Scroll sideways \(em by \fIN\fP columns, by half a screen, or so the cursor
sits at the left or right edge. These need soft wrap off; with it on there is
nowhere sideways to go.
.TP
.B "zA zC zO zD zF zj zk zv zx zX zi zn zN zm zr"
The rest of the folds: toggle/close/open recursively, delete a fold and all
nested in it, fold \fIN\fP lines, step to the next or previous fold, open just
enough to see the cursor, re\-apply the fold level, toggle
.RB ' foldenable ',
and lower or raise
.RB ' foldlevel '.
A fold is closed when its nesting depth exceeds the level;
.B zo
and
.B zc
set one fold directly and stand until the level moves again.
.TP
.B "zp zP zy"
Blockwise paste and yank without the trailing whitespace the ordinary
.BR p / P / y
add or keep.
.TP
.B zf\fImotion\fP
Fold the lines the motion covered into one row.
.B zo
opens the fold under the cursor,
.B zc
closes it,
.B za
toggles it,
.B zR
and
.B zM
open and close every fold, and
.B zd
and
.B zE
remove one or all. A closed fold counts as a single line to
.B j
and
.BR k .
Folding never changes the text, so it works on read-only buffers too.
.TP
.B ]q\fR, \fP[q
Walk the quickfix list, wrapping at both ends and taking a count. Fill it with
.B Ctrl-q
in the grep, references or diagnostics picker, which keeps every result
instead of choosing one.
.B :copen
shows the list in a split where
.B Enter
opens the entry under the cursor (in the window above, so the list stays);
.BR :cclose ", " :cnext ", " :cprev ", " :cfirst ", " :clast " and " ":cc {n}"
round it out, and
.B Space x
reaches the list from the leader tree.
.IP
.BR :cedit " (" "Space x e" ") opens the same list as a"
\fImultibuffer\fP: the two lines each side of every hit, stitched into one
ordinary \fIeditable\fP buffer under a
.B "\(em path:line"
header per excerpt, so a project\-wide change is made in one place and a
single
.B :w
writes every file it touched. Excerpts that would overlap \(em or merely
touch \(em are merged, so no source line is ever shown, or written back,
twice. The headers are the structure: whatever lies between two of them is
one excerpt's body, so lines may be added and removed freely, but a header
that was edited or deleted refuses the write rather than guessing. Each
excerpt also remembers the lines it was built from, so a file changed behind
the multibuffer's back is reported, not overwritten. The buffer is one
document of mixed languages and so renders without syntax highlighting; the
list contributes at most 200 excerpts, and
.B :w <name>
is refused \(em the stitched view belongs to no file.
.TP
.B "] [ \fR(space)\fP"
Add \fIN\fP blank lines below or above, leaving the cursor where it is.
.TP
.B "CTRL-A CTRL-X"
Add or subtract \fIN\fP from the number at or after the cursor. A leading
minus belongs to it,
.B 0x
makes it hexadecimal, and leading zeros keep the written width.
.TP
.B "CTRL-E CTRL-Y"
Scroll the window one line down or up. The cursor follows only when it would
otherwise leave the screen.
.TP
.B CTRL-G
Name the file, its line count and how far through it the cursor is.
.TP
.B CTRL-Z
Suspend to the shell. The terminal is handed back exactly as it was \(em raw
mode, the alternate screen, mouse reporting and the window background all
restored \(em and taken again when the shell resumes zedit with
.BR fg (1).
.TP
.B CTRL-^
Edit the alternate file \(em the buffer shown before this one.
.TP
.B "]' [' ]\` [\`"
Step to the next or previous mark \(em on its first non\-blank for the
quote forms, on its exact column for the backtick ones.
.TP
.B "]) [( ]} [{"
Leave the enclosing bracket, forwards or backwards. Nesting is counted, so a
balanced pair on the way is skipped.
.TP
.B "]] [[ ][ []"
Sections: a brace in column 0. With none in the file the motion runs to the
start or end of it.
.TP
.B "]/ [/ ]* [*"
The end of the next C comment, or the start of the previous one.
.TP
.B "]# [#"
The preprocessor conditional around the cursor, nesting counted.
.TP
.B "]p [p ]P [P"
Paste linewise with each line's indent replaced by the current line's.
.TP
.B "]m [m"
The brace that opens the next or previous member.
.TP
.B "]z [z"
The end or start of the fold the cursor is in.
.TP
.B "]c [c"
The next or previous changed hunk \(em vim's diff-mode motion, which here is
the same one
.B ]g
walks.
.TP
.B "]D [D ]Q [Q ]B [B"
The last or first diagnostic, quickfix entry or buffer, rather than the next.
.TP
.B ]d\fR, \fP[d\fR, \fP]e\fR, \fP[e\fR, \fP]w\fR, \fP[w
Walk diagnostics, wrapping and taking a count:
.BR ]d / [d
over every severity,
.BR ]e / [e
over errors only and
.BR ]w / [w
over warnings only.
.TP
.B ]g\fR, \fP[g
Walk the changed hunks of the file, wrapping at both ends. A hunk of any
length is a single stop.
.TP
.B Space n
New:
.B b
an empty buffer,
.B f
a new file,
.B d
a new folder. The file and folder prompts take a whole path, so
.I src/net/http.zig
creates both directories and the file — the same prompts the explorer's
.BR a / A
open, reachable without the tree.
.TP
.B Space S
This working directory's session:
.B s
save the open files, their cursors, the split layout and whether the tree was
open;
.B l
load it back;
.B d
delete it. Also
.BR ":session save|load|delete" .
Nothing is saved on exit or restored on launch — both directions are asked
for. A load refuses while any buffer is unsaved, and a file that has since
disappeared is skipped and counted rather than being fatal.
.TP
.B Space f u
The undo tree in a picker (what
.B :undolist
opens): every state, the current one marked, Enter jumps to it.
.TP
.B Space n
A new empty unnamed buffer in the active window (AstroNvim's
.BR <leader>n ).
The buffer it replaces stays open;
.B :w <name>
names it and starts highlighting and the language server.
.TP
.B Space u
UI toggles for this session (the config file is not touched):
.B n
relative numbers,
.B w
soft wrap,
.B d
inline diagnostics,
.B t
buffer tabs,
.B i
autoindent,
.B c
auto completion,
.B f
format on save,
.B m
mouse reporting.
.TP
.B Space b b\fR, \fPSpace b n\fR, \fPSpace b p\fR, \fPSpace b c
Buffers: the open\-buffer picker (same as
.BR "Space f b" ),
next buffer, previous buffer, close buffer (same as
.BR "Space c" ).
.TP
.B Space x q\fR, \fPSpace x n\fR, \fPSpace x p\fR, \fPSpace x c\fR, \fPSpace x e
Quickfix list: open it, next entry, previous entry, close it, and edit every
hit as one buffer (the multibuffer \(em see
.BR :cedit ).
.TP
.B Space h
Show the startup screen again (the recently\-opened list).
.TP
.B Space c\fR, \fPSpace w\fR, \fPSpace q
Close buffer, write, quit.
.PP
In a picker, type to filter,
.B Ctrl-n
/
.B Ctrl-p
or the arrow keys to move the selection,
.B Enter
to open, and
.B Esc
to cancel. A fuzzy query splits on spaces and every term must match
independently, in any order (helix\-style):
.I render editor
finds
.I src/editor/render.zig
as well as
.IR render_editor.zig ;
the scores add up, shorter candidates still win ties. The workspace\-symbol
picker is the other exception besides
.BR "Space f w" :
the language server does its matching, so the query reaches it verbatim,
spaces included. The in\-buffer
.B /
and
.B ?
search the current file; use
.B Space f w
to search across files.
.SH "COMMAND LINE"
.TP
.B :w \fR[\fPname\fR]\fP :q :wq :x :q! :qa :qa! :wa :format :ssh :update :{n} ZZ ZQ
Write, quit (closes the window when more than one is open, else quits; blocked
on the last window with unsaved changes), write\-and\-quit, force quit, quit
all (refuses while any buffer has unsaved changes;
.B :qa!
discards), write all dirty buffers (failed saves are reported by name),
go to line \fIn\fP, write\-and\-quit, quit without writing.
.TP
.B :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fP
A range on its own moves to its last line \(em
.BR :5 ", " :$ ", " "1,5" .
An address is
.BR % " (the whole file, i.e. " 1,$ "), " . " (the current line), " $
(the last),
a line number,
.BI ' mark
(including
.B '<
and
.B '>
for the last selection),
.BI / pattern /
or
.BI ? pattern ?
(the next or previous matching line), each optionally followed by
.BI + n
or
.BI \- n
offsets, which repeat and add up. Two addresses are joined by
.B ,
or by
.BR ; ,
which moves the cursor to the first before reading the second. An address
past the end of the buffer is clamped rather than refused, and a backwards
range is swapped without asking.
.TP
.B :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPd :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPy :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fP> :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fP< :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPj
Delete, yank, indent, dedent and join the range. Without one they act on the
cursor's line.
.TP
.B :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPg/pat/cmd\fR, \fP:\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPv/pat/cmd
Run \fIcmd\fP on every line of the range matching \fIpat\fP \(em or every line
\fInot\fP matching it, for
.BR :v .
The range defaults to the whole file. Every matching line is found before any
command runs, so a command that inserts or deletes lines cannot disturb the
search. With no \fIcmd\fP, the number of matching lines is reported.
.TP
.B :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPnormal\fR[\fP!\fR]\fP {keys}
Type \fIkeys\fP as though in normal mode: once at the cursor with no range,
once per line of the range from column 0 with one. An implicit
.B <Esc>
ends each run, so
.B :%normal A;
returns to normal mode.
.B !
is accepted and ignored \(em zedit has no mappings to suppress.
.TP
.B :\fR[\fPrange\fR]\fPs/pat/rep/\fR[\fPflags\fR]\fP
Substitute over the range (the current line without one). Flags:
.B g
(every occurrence on the line),
.B i
(ignore case). The replacement understands
.B &
(whole match) and
.BR \\e1 \- \\e9
(capture groups). Patterns are modern regexes (also used by
.BR / ):
.B . [..] * + ? ( ) | ^ $ \\ew \\ed \\es \\eb \\e< \\e>
\(em Helix-style, not vim's magic mode.
.TP
.B Tab\fR/\fPShift\-Tab
Complete the command line (nvim wildmenu semantics): command names, file
paths for
.BR :e / :w
(a unique directory completes with a trailing
.B /
and the next Tab descends into it) and
.B :theme
names. Only the text \fBbefore the cursor\fR is completed \(em the rest of
the line is put back after every candidate and the cursor lands between the
two (nvim). Multiple matches show a popup; Tab cycles matches then the text as
typed, Shift\-Tab cycles backward, and Left/Right select matches too (nvim's
wildmenu). While a path popup is open, Down descends into the selected
directory and re\-completes inside it, and Up re\-completes in the parent
directory; with any other popup, or none, Up/Down are history.
.TP
.B Up\fR/\fPDown\fR, \fPCtrl\-p\fR/\fPCtrl\-n
Command\-line history:
.B :
and
.BR / \| ?
keep separate histories. Up/Down recall entries starting with the text
already typed (vim's prefix filter); Down past the newest restores the typed
line; Ctrl\-p/Ctrl\-n recall without filtering. Recall leaves the cursor at
the end of the line (vim).
.TP
.B Left\fR/\fPRight\fR, \fPHome\fR/\fPEnd\fR, \fPCtrl\-b\fR/\fPCtrl\-e
Move the cursor within the command line (vim's cmdline keys): typed and
pasted text inserts at the cursor, backspace deletes before it (an empty
line still cancels). A line wider than the terminal wraps onto further screen
rows \(em the command\-line area grows upward over the window, as nvim's does
\(em rather than scrolling sideways. A double\-width character that would
straddle the edge starts the next row, and the cell it leaves over is marked
.BR > ,
as in vim.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the character under the cursor; at the end of the line the one before
it, and on an empty line it cancels the command line (vim's
.BR c_<Del> ).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-w
Erase the word before the cursor, the whitespace in front of it included
(vim's
.BR c_CTRL\-W ).
A run of punctuation counts as a word of its own, and CJK never merges with
ASCII. A no\-op at column 0.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-u
Erase everything between the start of the line and the cursor, keeping the
tail (vim's
.BR c_CTRL\-U ).
Unlike backspace it never cancels the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-r {register}
Insert a register's contents at the cursor (vim's
.BR c_CTRL\-R ):
.BR a \- z ,
the unnamed
.BR \(dq ,
and the clipboard
.BR + / * .
A
.B \(dq
is drawn at the cursor while the register name is awaited; Esc there
abandons the prompt and keeps the line, and an unknown or empty register
inserts nothing. A register holding several lines inserts one separator per
line break and drops the trailing one; the text is untrusted, so control
bytes render as
.B ?
and never reach the terminal live.
.TP
.B Right\fR/\fPEnd\fR at end\-of\-line
Accept the inline suggestion (fish\-style ghost text): while typing, the rest
of the newest history entry extending the typed text \(em or, for
.BR : ,
the first matching command name \(em is shown dimmed after the cursor.
Mid\-line the keys only move the cursor and the suggestion hides until the
cursor returns to the end (fish's rule).
Enter always runs only what you typed; the suggestion hides while the Tab
wildmenu is open, and the rename prompt never shows one. Disable with
.B cmdline_suggestions = false
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.SH "BUFFERS & WINDOWS"
Several files can be open at once, each with its own cursor, undo history and
language server.
.TP
.B :e \fIfile\fP
Open \fIfile\fP in the active window (reusing it if already open).
.TP
.B :bn :bp :bd :ls
Next / previous buffer in the active window, close the current buffer, list
open buffers. Closing the last buffer replaces it with an empty [No Name]
one and the window stays, as in vim; a buffer with unsaved changes refuses
unless forced with
.BR :bd! ,
which discards them.
.TP
.B ]b [b
Cycle to the next / previous buffer; a count skips
.RB ( 2]b ).
.TP
.B :split :vsplit :close :only
Split the view horizontally / vertically, close the active window, close all
but the active window.
.TP
.B :theme \fR[\fPname\fR]\fP
Switch the colour theme (with no argument, opens the theme picker; also on
.BR "Space f t" ).
.TP
.B Ctrl-h Ctrl-j Ctrl-k Ctrl-l
Move focus one window in that direction (AstroNvim's window keys). The file
tree counts as the window beyond the edge it is docked to, so
.B Ctrl-h
steps into the explorer and
.B Ctrl-l
steps back out. They do not wrap.
.TP
.B Ctrl-w \fR{\fPv s c o w h j k l + \- < > =\fR}\fP
Window commands: \fBv\fP/\fBs\fP split vertically/horizontally, \fBc\fP close,
\fBo\fP only, \fBw\fP/\fBh\fP/\fBj\fP/\fBk\fP/\fBl\fP move focus between windows.
\fB+\fP/\fB\-\fP make the focused window taller/shorter and \fB<\fP/\fB>\fP
narrower/wider, by one cell or by a count (\fB5 Ctrl-w +\fP); \fB=\fP tiles
them evenly again. The difference is taken from the other windows, none of
which is squeezed below a single cell. See
.B split_sizes
and
.B :winsave
for keeping a layout between sessions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
Used for terminal capabilities. A Nerd Font is recommended so the powerline
statusline separators render correctly.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (e.g. not a terminal, or a file could not be opened).
.TP
.B 2
Command\-line usage error.
